        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which EcoFlow Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the job, not the battery. Most bad EcoFlow purchases come from buying the biggest box the budget allows and then discovering it is too heavy to take anywhere, or from buying a River for an outage and finding out it will not run the refrigerator. Match the model to the heaviest thing you actually need to power, and to how often you will move it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phones, laptops and a router during short outages</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>River 3</strong>. Its 300W output and 245Wh battery keep a modem, router and a couple of laptops running for several hours, and it costs little enough to leave plugged in as an always-on backup. If you think you will want to run a small fan, a CPAP or a 12V cooler as well, step up to the River 3 Plus instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Camping, van trips and CPAP nights</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>River 3 Plus</strong>. It is the lightest EcoFlow that runs a CPAP comfortably and the only small model with an expansion battery. Weekend campers who also want to keep food cold should read our guide to the <a href="/best-12v-fridge-for-camping/">best 12V fridge for camping</a>, because an efficient compressor fridge is what makes a small battery last. If your camping includes an induction plate or an electric kettle, you have outgrown the River line and want a Delta.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fridge, freezer and essentials through an outage</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Delta 3 Plus</strong>, and add an extra battery if a single kilowatt-hour turns out to be tight. This is the right answer for most households: it starts a refrigerator, runs a microwave, recharges in under an hour when the power comes back, and is still light enough to take camping. If you want 2kWh from day one and can find the <strong>Delta 2 Max</strong> on a good sale, it is the cheaper way to get there in one box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Circuits in the house, a furnace or a 240V pump</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Delta Pro 3</strong> and budget for an electrician. It is the smallest EcoFlow with 240V output and the first one that makes sense connected to the house rather than to extension cords. Only if you are planning a permanent, whole-home installation with automatic switchover should you look at the <strong>Delta Pro Ultra</strong>, and at that price you should also compare it against a standby unit in our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="river-vs-delta" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. River 3, River 3 Plus or Delta 3 Plus?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These three are the models most shoppers are actually choosing between, and the gaps between them are larger than the names suggest. The River 3 and River 3 Plus look like siblings, but the Plus has twice the inverter output and an expansion port. The Delta 3 Plus is not a bigger River; it is a different class of machine with three times the output and nearly four times the storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful dividing line is the refrigerator. A full-size kitchen fridge draws only 100 to 200 watts while running, but its compressor can pull several times that for a moment at start-up, and it uses roughly 1 to 2kWh a day. Neither River can carry that. The River 3 Plus might technically start a small, efficient fridge, but its 286Wh battery would be empty within a few hours. The Delta 3 Plus, with 1,024Wh and 1,800W, runs a typical fridge for something like 10 to 20 hours depending on the model and how often the door opens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below that line, the River 3 Plus is the better buy than the River 3 for almost everyone. The extra 300 watts covers a CPAP with some headroom, a small electric cooler, a projector or a modest power tool charger, and the expansion battery means you are not stuck if your needs grow. The River 3 makes sense only when the budget is tight and the load list is strictly electronics, or when you want a second small unit for a home office.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above it, the Delta 3 Plus is the most versatile EcoFlow by a wide margin. At about 28 pounds it is heavier than either River but still a one-person carry, which means the same box can keep the fridge cold during an outage and run the coffee maker at a campsite. That flexibility is why it tops this list. If you are deciding between two Rivers for different rooms and one Delta for the house, the single Delta is usually the better use of the money.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: EcoFlow also sells a plain <strong>Delta 3</strong> without the Plus, at a lower price. It shares the same basic capacity class but trims some charging and input features. Compare the spec sheets line by line on the day you buy, because the gap in price is not always as large as the gap in features.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="expansion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Expansion Batteries: Buying Capacity Later
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expansion is the strongest argument for EcoFlow over Jackery's v2 Explorer line. Most EcoFlow models from the River 3 Plus upward have a port for a matched extra battery, so you can buy the base unit now and add storage when you find out how much you really use. Jackery's popular Explorer v2 boxes are fixed in size; outgrowing one means selling it or buying a second station.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pattern is consistent across the range. The River 3 Plus takes a snap-on extra battery that roughly triples its capacity. The Delta 3 Plus pairs with EcoFlow's Delta 3-series extra batteries to reach about 5kWh by EcoFlow's figures. The Delta 2 Max reaches about 6kWh with its own extra batteries. The Delta Pro 3 grows into the tens of kilowatt-hours, and the Delta Pro Ultra is built from stackable 6kWh modules from the start.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are two things to understand before you plan around this. First, expansion adds storage, not output. A Delta 3 Plus with two extra batteries still has an 1,800W inverter, so it runs the same appliances for longer; it does not suddenly start a 240V pump. If you need more watts, you need a bigger base unit. Second, extra batteries are model-specific. A battery designed for one generation may not work with another, so buy the base unit with the expansion line you intend to use, and check compatibility on EcoFlow's own listing rather than a reseller's description.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expansion batteries also cost real money, often a large share of the base unit's price per kilowatt-hour. For buyers who know from day one that they want 2kWh or more, a single larger unit is frequently cheaper than a small unit plus batteries. The expansion route earns its keep for people who are unsure, who want a lighter box to carry camping and a larger stack at home, or who are building toward a home system in stages.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for the watts you need now; add batteries for the hours you need later.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Output cannot be upgraded after purchase. Storage can. Pick the base unit by its inverter and grow the runtime with extra batteries.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Home Backup: Smart Home Panel, Transfer Switches and 240V
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most EcoFlow units back up a house the simple way: you run extension cords from the station to the refrigerator, the router and a lamp. That works with any model and needs no electrician. It also means the furnace, the well pump and anything hardwired stays off, because those loads cannot be plugged into an outlet. To power circuits rather than cords, you need a station that can feed the house panel safely, and in EcoFlow's range that starts with the Delta Pro models.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Delta Pro 3 delivers 120V and 240V natively, which matters because many of the loads you most want during an outage are 240V or sit on circuits you cannot reach with a cord. Connected through a manual transfer switch or an interlock and generator inlet, installed by a licensed electrician, it can run the circuits you choose, such as the fridge, the furnace blower, a few lighting circuits and the network closet. Never backfeed a house through a dryer outlet or a so-called suicide cord. It is illegal, and it can kill a lineworker.
          </p>

          <p>
            EcoFlow's own answer for a cleaner install is its Smart Home Panel 2, a circuit-level transfer panel that switches over automatically when the grid fails and lets you see and control each circuit in the app. It is built around the Delta Pro-class systems, and it is the natural partner for the Delta Pro Ultra. Compatibility has shifted between product generations, so confirm on EcoFlow's site that the panel and the station you are buying work together before you order either. A smart panel adds meaningfully to the cost and requires professional installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what you actually need is a 240V well pump, read our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-for-well-pump/">best generator for a well pump</a> before choosing; starting surge on a deep-well pump is the load most likely to defeat a battery. And if you want the station to protect sensitive electronics during the transfer, pair the installation with one of the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protectors</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a broader look at how power stations compare to permanent options, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power stations for home backup</a> guide sets the Delta Pro 3 against Anker SOLIX and Jackery's large units, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">best home battery backup</a> systems covers wall-mounted batteries that compete with the Delta Pro Ultra.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Solar Input and EcoFlow Solar Generator Bundles
          </h2>

          <p>
            An EcoFlow solar generator is not a different machine. It is one of the stations above sold together with one or more EcoFlow folding or rigid panels and the cable to connect them. The station inside the box is identical to the one sold alone, so the questions are the same: is the station the right size, and is the panel bundle large enough to refill it?
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful rule of thumb is one watt of panel for every three watt-hours you want back on a good day, because a portable panel rarely delivers its full rating for more than a few hours. By that rule a 100W panel refills most of a River 3 Plus on a clear day, a 200W panel does it with margin, and the Delta 3 Plus wants something like 300 to 400W of panels to be refilled daily. The Delta Pro 3 needs a permanent array in the kilowatt range before solar is a real recharge path rather than a top-up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common bundle mistake is buying a large station with a single folding panel. A Delta Pro 3 with one 400W panel is, for practical purposes, a battery you can only refill from the wall. It is better to buy a smaller station with enough panel to refill it than a larger one with token solar. Larger Delta models have two solar inputs, which lets you add panels in stages; the River models have one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stay inside the station's voltage and current window. Every EcoFlow lists a solar input range on its spec sheet, and exceeding the voltage limit, especially on a cold, bright morning when panel voltage rises, can damage the charge controller. EcoFlow's own panels pair without any math, but third-party panels work fine if the connector and voltage fit. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> works through the panel sizing for each station class, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> covers folding panels that fit EcoFlow inputs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mostly travel by vehicle, also look at EcoFlow's 800W Alternator Charger, which refills a Delta-class station from the engine while you drive. For overlanders and van owners it is often a faster and more reliable recharge source than a roof panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="app-charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The EcoFlow App, UPS Mode and Charging Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every current EcoFlow connects to the EcoFlow app over Wi-Fi or Bluetooth, and the app is more useful than most. It shows input and output watts in real time, estimates remaining runtime, lets you set charge limits to extend battery life, and controls the AC and DC outputs remotely. On the larger units it also manages solar priority, schedules and, on Delta Pro-class systems, circuit-level control through the smart panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downside is that some settings you will want live only in the app. Charging speed, battery charge limits and some output modes are easier to change from a phone than from the unit itself. If you are buying for a parent who will never open an app, that is a mark against EcoFlow and in favor of a simpler Jackery, though the stations work out of the box with default settings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging speed is where EcoFlow made its name. The River 3 series and the Delta 3 Plus are quoted at around an hour to full from a household outlet, which matters more than it sounds. In a rolling outage or a storm with intermittent power, a station that refills in an hour during a short window of grid power can be the difference between a full battery and an empty one at night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most current EcoFlow models also work as a basic uninterruptible power supply. Plug the station into the wall and your router, modem or desktop into the station, and EcoFlow quotes a switchover in the range of 10 milliseconds or less when the grid drops. That is fast enough for most home electronics, though a dedicated <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">UPS battery backup</a> is still the better tool for a desktop computer or network rack that cannot tolerate any interruption.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-ecoflow-loses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Where EcoFlow Loses (and What Not to Buy)
          </h2>

          <p>
            EcoFlow is our default recommendation in most size classes, but not every model is worth buying and the brand is not best at everything. Weight is the clearest weakness. At 1kWh and 2kWh, Jackery's Explorer v2 boxes store similar energy in lighter cases, and for campers who carry the station from the car every weekend, that difference adds up. Our <a href="/best-jackery-power-station/">Jackery power station guide</a> covers those models in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Output per dollar at the very top is another. Anker's SOLIX F3800 offers more inverter output than the Delta Pro 3 in a similar class, and Bluetti competes hard on price at every size. If your shortlist is EcoFlow against Bluetti, our <a href="/ecoflow-vs-bluetti/">EcoFlow vs Bluetti</a> comparison works through the differences, and our <a href="/best-anker-solix-power-station/">Anker SOLIX guide</a> covers Anker's large units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within EcoFlow's own range, skip older NMC models still sold through third-party listings, such as the original Delta and River models, unless the discount is steep and the use is occasional. LFP is the better chemistry for a backup battery, and the newer models charge faster and integrate better with the app. Be wary, too, of listings that bundle a large station with one small panel and call it a solar generator; buy the station and panels to fit each other instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            And do not buy the Delta Pro Ultra as a portable power station. It is a home battery system. If you will not wire it into the house with a smart panel, a Delta Pro 3 or two gives you most of the capability for far less money and can still roll out to the driveway when needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Long Each EcoFlow Will Run Your Gear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Capacity on the box is not capacity at the outlet. Inverter losses and battery management mean a station typically delivers something like 80 to 90 percent of its rated watt-hours as AC power. EcoFlow's own figure for the Delta Pro 3, about 3,480Wh usable from 4,096Wh, is a good illustration. Plan with roughly 85 percent of the label and you will rarely be disappointed.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a CPAP without the heated humidifier, drawing around 30 to 60 watts, the River 3 Plus covers roughly one night, the River 3 slightly less, and the Delta 3 Plus several nights. Turn the humidifier on and consumption can double or triple, which is the most common reason a small station runs out before morning. Our guide to the <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">best battery backup for CPAP</a> goes through the math by machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a refrigerator, think in days rather than hours. A typical modern fridge uses 1 to 2kWh a day, so the Delta 3 Plus covers most of a day, the Delta 2 Max about a day, the Delta Pro 3 about two days, and a Delta Pro Ultra with a single battery module three days or more if nothing else is running. Add a chest freezer, a furnace blower or a sump pump and those numbers shrink quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For electronics, the River line goes further than you might expect. A router and modem together typically draw 15 to 30 watts, so even the River 3 keeps a home office online for several hours. Phones and tablets barely register. If those are your only outage needs, a station may be more than you need at all, and our roundup of <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> covers lighter, cheaper options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a River for a refrigerator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The River 3 and River 3 Plus are built for electronics, CPAP machines and small coolers. A kitchen refrigerator uses one to two kilowatt-hours a day, which empties a River in a few hours even if it starts the compressor. If keeping food cold is the goal, the Delta 3 Plus is the smallest EcoFlow that makes sense.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting extra batteries to add output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Expansion batteries lengthen runtime but leave the inverter alone. A Delta 3 Plus with two extra batteries still tops out at 1,800W and still has no 240V output. Choose the base unit by the biggest load you need to start, then add batteries for the hours you need to cover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing a big station with one small panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 4kWh Delta Pro 3 bundled with a single folding panel will take days of sun to refill. Size solar to the battery, roughly one watt of panel per three watt-hours you want back daily, or treat the station as wall-charged and skip the bundle premium entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding the house through an outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a station into a dryer or wall outlet with a male-to-male cord to power the house is illegal and can electrocute utility workers. Use extension cords to individual appliances, or have an electrician install a transfer switch, interlock or EcoFlow's smart panel for a Delta Pro-class unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the solar voltage limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every EcoFlow lists a maximum solar input voltage. Wiring third-party panels in series without checking can exceed it, especially on cold, sunny mornings when panel voltage rises above its rating. Check the combined open-circuit voltage of the whole string against the spec sheet before connecting anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

