            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which EcoFlow power station is best for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Delta 3 Plus. EcoFlow rates it at 1,024Wh and 1,800W, which starts a refrigerator and runs a microwave, and it accepts extra batteries if 1kWh proves too small. At about 28 pounds it is still portable enough for camping. Smaller households focused on electronics can save money with the River 3 Plus instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between EcoFlow River and Delta?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                River models are small, light power stations for electronics, CPAP machines and camping, with a few hundred watts of output and under 300Wh of built-in storage. Delta models start around 1kWh with 1,800W or more of output, enough for kitchen appliances and refrigerators, and most accept add-on batteries. Delta Pro models add 240V for home backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an EcoFlow solar generator different from the power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. An EcoFlow solar generator is one of its power stations sold in a bundle with folding or rigid panels and cables. The station is identical to the one sold alone. Judge a bundle by whether the panel wattage is large enough to refill the battery in a day, roughly one watt of panel per three watt-hours of storage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an EcoFlow power station run my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on a single portable unit. A Delta Pro 3 with 4kWh and 240V output can run selected essential circuits through a transfer switch, and a Delta Pro Ultra with several battery modules and the Smart Home Panel 2 approaches whole-home backup. Central air, electric ranges and electric water heaters will still drain any of them quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy the Delta 3 Plus or the Delta Pro 3?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy the Delta 3 Plus if you mainly need a fridge, internet and lights on extension cords, or want one unit for outages and camping. Buy the Delta Pro 3 if you need 240V, want to power house circuits through a transfer switch, or plan a large solar array. The Pro costs several times as much and weighs over 100 pounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do EcoFlow power stations work without the app?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every model works out of the box with its physical buttons and default settings, so you can plug in and go. The app adds real-time monitoring, charge limits, charging-speed control and remote output switching, and some settings are only reachable through it. Most owners install it once to set things up and then rarely open it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do EcoFlow batteries last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Current EcoFlow models use LFP cells, which EcoFlow rates for thousands of charge cycles before capacity falls significantly. For a station used occasionally for outages and camping, that means the battery is likely to outlast its electronics. Store it at partial charge in a cool place and top it up every few months between uses to keep it healthy.
              </p>
